})
```

This keeps SQL visible in dev for debugging but suppresses it in prod/staging.

## Severity: HIGH

## Summary
SCIM token validation uses standard string equality (==) instead of a constant-time comparison, making it vulnerable to timing attacks.

## Finding
**File:** src/Modules/Identity/Foundry.Identity.Infrastructure/Services/ScimService.cs:157-167

```csharp
public async Task<bool> ValidateTokenAsync(string token, CancellationToken ct = default)
{
    // ...
    string hashedToken = HashToken(token);
    return config.BearerToken == hashedToken;  // <-- Standard string comparison
}
```

Note: The API key validation (RedisApiKeyService) avoids this by using hash-based lookup (hash the key, look up by hash in Redis), which is inherently timing-safe. But SCIM token comparison is a direct string comparison.

## Impact
An attacker could use timing side-channels to recover the token hash byte-by-byte, potentially allowing SCIM API access.

## Required Fix
Use `CryptographicOperations.FixedTimeEquals()` for the comparison. Convert both hash strings to byte arrays (UTF-8) and compare with the constant-time method:
```csharp
byte[] expectedBytes = Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(config.BearerToken);
byte[] actualBytes = Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(hashedToken);
return CryptographicOperations.FixedTimeEquals(expectedBytes, actualBytes);
```

notes:
SCOUT: all citations verified EXACT — Program.cs:177 enabledModules, :693-694 apiKeyFeatureCheck.IsEnabledAsync("Modules.ApiKeys"), :749-751 jobFeatureManager.IsEnabledAsync("Modules.Notifications"). No file/type collision found with the other 8 children beyond sharing the read-only enabledModules list with Wallow-3mbx.1's option B fix (different Program.cs regions, no hard edge — see epic note). Free-standing; can be picked up any time. Fix is small: replace both IsEnabledAsync("Modules.X") re-resolutions with a lookup against enabledModules (e.g. an extension method), no BuildServiceProvider/IFeatureManager re-resolution needed at either site.

DESIGN: Verified premise, re-derived against the CURRENT tree (3mbx.1 landed as commit 4f9b0f10, scul in flight uncommitted). One structural fix, no runtime IFeatureManager reads left in Program.cs.

=== 1. VERIFIED STATE / CITATION DRIFT ===

Exactly 2 sites, count confirmed via `grep -n 'IsEnabledAsync\|"Modules\.' api/src/Wallow.Api/Program.cs`. Lines have DRIFTED from the bead's citation (693-694/749-751) — current tree has them at 701-702/757-759 (scul's own DESIGN REVIEW note already flagged this same drift independently; corroborated here by direct grep, not trusted from that note):

  Program.cs:701-702
    IFeatureManager apiKeyFeatureCheck = app.Services.GetRequiredService<IFeatureManager>();
    if (await apiKeyFeatureCheck.IsEnabledAsync("Modules.ApiKeys"))
        app.UseMiddleware<ApiKeyAuthenticationMiddleware>();          // inside a `{ }` block at 700-706

  Program.cs:757-759
    IFeatureManager jobFeatureManager = jobScope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<IFeatureManager>();
    if (await jobFeatureManager.IsEnabledAsync("Modules.Notifications"))
        jobManager.AddOrUpdate<RetryFailedEmailsJob>(...)             // inside `await using (AsyncServiceScope jobScope = ...)` at 748-766

No third site. `builder.Services.AddFeatureManagement()` at line 539 is registration, not a read — leave it; IFeatureManager stays needed by ResolveEnabledModules (WallowModules.cs), so `using Microsoft.FeatureManagement;` stays even after this fix.

=== 2. Wallow-3mbx.1 CHECKED — landed clean, no third string-literal reader, no reusable helper to pick up ===

Read the actual landed diff (commit 4f9b0f10), not the bead's mid-session design notes (which explored and rejected an `ApiAssembly`-on-`IWallowModule` idea and a namespace-regex idea before landing on a third approach). What shipped is a module-name-keyed table in WallowModules.cs:

  private static readonly (string ModuleName, Assembly ApiAssembly)[] _moduleApiAssemblies = [ ("Identity", typeof(UsersController).Assembly), ... ];
  internal static void RemoveDisabledModuleApiParts(ApplicationPartManager manager, IReadOnlyList<IWallowModule> enabledModules)

This is NOT an IFeatureManager/"Modules.X" string read — it keys module identity by plain `IWallowModule.Name` strings ("Identity", "ApiKeys", ...), same convention `Name`'s own XML doc already states ("Storage is gated by FeatureManagement:Modules.Storage" — IWallowModule.cs:22-24) and the same convention this design's new helper (below) uses. `RemoveDisabledModuleApiParts` consumes `enabledModules` directly, exactly like this design's fix does — confirms the pattern is now precedented in this file, not novel. It built no reusable "is module X enabled" helper itself (its own HashSet<string> enabledNames is built and discarded inline once per call), so there is nothing to reuse from it — this design adds the first one.

=== 3. THE FIX ===

3.1. NEW METHOD in api/src/Wallow.Api/WallowModules.cs, placed directly after `RemoveDisabledModuleApiParts` (before `EnsureEveryShippedModuleIsMapped`):

    /// <summary>
    /// True when <paramref name="enabledModules"/> — the exact set <c>AddWallowModules</c> registered —
    /// contains a module named <paramref name="moduleName"/>.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The one place left in <c>Wallow.Api</c> that still needs to ask "is module X on" outside
    /// <c>AddWallowModules</c> itself. Checks membership in the already-resolved set rather than
    /// re-resolving <see cref="IFeatureManager"/> and re-deriving the <c>"Modules.{Name}"</c> flag key,
    /// so a module the registry forces on regardless of its flag (<see cref="IWallowModule.IsCore"/>)
    /// reads as enabled here too — <c>enabledModules</c> already applied that short-circuit in
    /// <c>ResolveEnabledModules</c>; this method never re-derives it.
    /// </remarks>
    internal static bool IsModuleEnabled(this IReadOnlyList<IWallowModule> enabledModules, string moduleName)
    {
        ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(enabledModules);
        return enabledModules.Any(module => string.Equals(module.Name, moduleName, StringComparison.Ordinal));
    }

3.2. Program.cs:700-706 becomes:

    // API key authentication (checks X-Api-Key header first, falls through to JWT if not present)
    // Only register when ApiKeys module is enabled — the middleware depends on IApiKeyService
    if (enabledModules.IsModuleEnabled("ApiKeys"))
    {
        app.UseMiddleware<ApiKeyAuthenticationMiddleware>();
    }

  Delete the `apiKeyFeatureCheck` local and the wrapping `{ }` scope block entirely (no longer needed once there is only the one statement inside).

3.3. Program.cs:757-759 becomes:

    if (enabledModules.IsModuleEnabled("Notifications"))
    {
        jobManager.AddOrUpdate<RetryFailedEmailsJob>(
            "retry-failed-emails",
            job => job.ExecuteAsync(CancellationToken.None),
            "*/5 * * * *");
    }

  Delete the `jobFeatureManager` local. `enabledModules` is a plain top-level local in scope for the rest of the file — no DI resolution needed at this call site either.

Net effect on Program.cs: zero remaining `"Modules.` string literals (bead's own acceptance grep now returns nothing), zero remaining `IFeatureManager`-typed locals.

=== 4. ORDERING / LIFETIME — verified, not assumed ===

`enabledModules` (type `IReadOnlyList<IWallowModule>`) is assigned once at Program.cs:177, inside the same top-level `try` block both target sites live in. Read the actual file end-to-end (not just the two call sites) to confirm no local function or lambda boundary sits between 177 and 759 that would hide it: the two edit sites are plain nested `{ }` / `await using (...)` blocks in the SAME method body — nested blocks do not create a new variable scope in C#, so `enabledModules` is visible at both 701-702 and 757-759 exactly as it already is at Program.cs:507 (3mbx.1's own `ConfigureApplicationPartManager(manager => WallowModules.RemoveDisabledModuleApiParts(manager, enabledModules))` call, which already proves the same list is usable this far into the method). No availability problem exists; nothing to design around here.

=== 5. SEAM FOR Wallow-3mbx.4 ===

3mbx.4 rewrites `ResolveEnabledModules`'s internals (WallowModules.cs, currently the `BuildServiceProvider`/`GetAwaiter().GetResult()` method) but not its signature or `AddWallowModules`'s return type. This design's new `IsModuleEnabled` never calls `ResolveEnabledModules`, `IFeatureManager`, or `IConfiguration` — it only reads the already-computed `IReadOnlyList<IWallowModule>`. Zero file-region or behavioral coupling; 3mbx.4 can land before, after, or in parallel with this bead. Do not fold 3mbx.4's `ResolveEnabledModules` rewrite into this bead — out of scope here.

=== 6. TEST PLAN — structural + behavioral, no source-reading ===

The fix is structural in the sense that matters here: after 3.2/3.3 there is no code path left in Program.cs that can re-derive a "Modules.X" string and disagree with the registry — both call sites now read the one list every other consumer already reads. What is NOT structurally preventable in C# (no linter reaches this repo's C#, per prompt) is a *future* re-introduction of an `IFeatureManager.IsEnabledAsync("Modules.Y")` call somewhere else in Program.cs — that is a code-review concern, not something a test can foreclose, and the bead's own acceptance grep is the intended human/CI tripwire for it (do NOT write a test that great `File.ReadAllText`s Program.cs — that is exactly the banned source-reading-test shape per .claude/rules/TESTING.md; the grep belongs in the commit description / a human check, never in a spec).

What a test CAN and should prove: that `IsModuleEnabled` has the exact membership semantics the fix relies on, and that the two call sites' externally-observable behavior is unchanged.

6.1. FAST UNIT TEST — new file api/tests/Wallow.Api.Tests/WallowModulesIsModuleEnabledTests.cs (or add to WallowModulesApplicationPartsTests.cs; that file already declares a private `StubModule(string name) : IWallowModule` fixture at lines 96-113 — reuse it verbatim, do not redeclare):

    [Fact]
    public void IsModuleEnabled_ReturnsTrue_WhenTheNamedModuleIsInTheList()
    {
        IReadOnlyList<IWallowModule> enabledModules = [new StubModule("Storage")];
        enabledModules.IsModuleEnabled("Storage").Should().BeTrue();
    }

    [Fact]
    public void IsModuleEnabled_ReturnsFalse_WhenTheNamedModuleIsAbsent()
    {
        IReadOnlyList<IWallowModule> enabledModules = [new StubModule("Storage")];
        enabledModules.IsModuleEnabled("ApiKeys").Should().BeFalse();
    }

    [Fact]
    public void IsModuleEnabled_ReturnsFalse_ForAnEmptyList()
    {
        IReadOnlyList<IWallowModule> enabledModules = [];
        enabledModules.IsModuleEnabled("Notifications").Should().BeFalse();
