design:
CRITICAL fork-safety requirement: api/branding.json is merge=ours in .gitattributes, and packages/styles/src/branding.ts's toCssVars() drops empty values (ThemeColors is Readonly<Record<string,string>>). A fork whose branding.json predates these keys will emit NO --sidebar custom property at all. Every new @theme mapping in packages/styles/styles.css MUST therefore carry a fallback, e.g.:
  --color-sidebar: var(--sidebar, var(--foreground));
  --color-sidebar-foreground: var(--sidebar-foreground, var(--background));
  --color-sidebar-accent: var(--sidebar-accent, var(--accent));
  --color-success: var(--success, var(--primary));
  --color-success-foreground: var(--success-foreground, var(--primary-foreground));
Pick sensible same-purpose fallbacks (shown above) so an old fork's build still resolves to something visually coherent rather than 'currentcolor' or transparent.

Existing @theme block reference (packages/styles/styles.css lines 17-40): it currently maps background, foreground, card, card-foreground, popover, popover-foreground, primary, primary-foreground, secondary, secondary-foreground, muted, muted-foreground, accent, accent-foreground, destructive, destructive-foreground, border, input, ring, plus radius-lg/md/sm. Add the five new --color-* lines in that same block, none of them need a fallback pattern change to existing tokens -- only the five NEW ones need the var(new, fallback) two-level indirection since the OLD tokens (--color-background: var(--background);) have no fallback today and must not gain one (that would be an unrelated behavioural change to a well-tested block).

Do NOT change any existing token's value.

Theme tests live in packages/styles/src/ (grep the package for a file matching *theme-css*.test.ts or similar -- it is catalog-derived from the @theme block and the branding.json theme keys, so it will fail if the two sides drift). Read that test file first to understand its exact assertion shape before writing the fallback CSS.

