notes:
Filed from the bead backlog audit of 2026-08-02 (docs/audits/2026-08-02/0824-bead-backlog), finding L4. Re-derived while filing and wider than the audit recorded: appsettings.json carries a SECOND modules block under Wallow:Modules with the same phantom Configuration entry and zero readers anywhere in api/src — the audit records only the FeatureManagement block. Coupled to Wallow-vmns.7, which is deriving ModuleToggleTests' hardcoded key list; that list currently includes Modules.Identity, a toggle that does nothing.
Wallow-juxf (drop the phantom Modules.Configuration flag) is a strict subset of this bead — collapse or dep-link when picking either up (found during 2026-08-04 doc-audit bead reconciliation).
SCOUT: citations verified. appsettings.json FeatureManagement block (lines 80-89) declares Modules.Branding/Identity/Storage/Notifications/Announcements/Configuration/Inquiries/ApiKeys (8 keys) — confirmed. WallowModules.cs (grepped) reads Modules.{Name} for the 6 non-core modules via ResolveEnabledModules — Identity is unconditional (IsCore), Configuration has zero readers anywhere in api/src, confirmed both. Second duplicate block: appsettings.json Wallow:Modules (lines 91-99, Identity/Notifications/Announcements/Storage/Configuration/Inquiries/ApiKeys) — grepped for a reader of "Wallow:Modules" or "Wallow__Modules": zero hits, confirmed genuinely dead. appsettings.Production.json mirrors the FeatureManagement block exactly including the phantom key (lines 48-56) — confirmed. BLOCKS Wallow-juxf (bd dep add Wallow-juxf Wallow-scul already wired) — this bead's acceptance criteria is a strict superset of juxf's; do this one and juxf becomes a closeable duplicate. ModuleToggleTests.cs's CoreModule_ShouldStillRegister_WhenFeatureFlagDisabled test (api/tests/Wallow.Architecture.Tests/Modules/ModuleToggleTests.cs:16-42) currently sets FeatureManagement:Modules.Identity=false and asserts Identity still registers — confirmed this exercises exactly the "Identity flag does nothing" behavior scul must decide on (delete the key or make it honoured); whichever choice is made, this test's config dictionary needs to stay consistent with it.

DESIGN: Verified premise, decided juxf=SUBSUMED (close as dup once this lands), and committed to one reconciliation+enforcement design. Re-derived against the CURRENT tree — k2zp/lw4a already collapsed WallowModules.cs's per-module registration into a generic registry-driven loop, and 3mbx.1's fix is DONE BUT UNCOMMITTED in the working tree (git status: modified api/src/Wallow.Api/{WallowModules.cs,Program.cs}, untracked api/tests/Wallow.Api.Tests/{WallowModulesApplicationPartsTests.cs,Integration/DisabledModuleHttpSurfaceTests.cs}). scul's original body cites "WallowModules.cs:45-46" per-module hardcoding that no longer exists — flagging that drift here per instructions, not editing the bead body.

=== 1. VERIFIED STATE (current tree, 2026-08-05) ===

api/src/Wallow.Api/appsettings.json FeatureManagement (lines 80-89) declares 8 keys: Modules.Branding/Identity/Storage/Notifications/Announcements/Configuration/Inquiries/ApiKeys. Same file's Wallow:Modules (lines 90-100) duplicates 7 of them (no ApiKeys... wait it has ApiKeys too) with the same Configuration phantom. appsettings.Production.json FeatureManagement (lines 48-57) mirrors the 8-key set exactly (ApiKeys=true there vs false in base). appsettings.Development.json (line 76-78) overrides only Modules.ApiKeys=true. appsettings.Staging.json and appsettings.Testing.json declare ZERO Modules.* keys (grepped, confirmed) — not part of the drift surface, no edit needed.

Reader, re-derived from current code (NOT the bead's stale per-module citation): WallowModules.ResolveEnabledModules (api/src/Wallow.Api/WallowModules.cs:207-218) is now one generic loop: `WallowModuleRegistry.All.Where(module => module.IsCore || featureManager.IsEnabledAsync($"Modules.{module.Name}").GetAwaiter().GetResult())`. Because `||` short-circuits, IsEnabledAsync is NEVER invoked for a core module (today: only Identity). So:
  - Modules.Configuration: phantom. No module named Configuration exists in WallowModuleRegistry.All (api/src/Wallow.Modules.Registry/WallowModuleRegistry.cs:23-32, 7 entries: Identity, Branding, Notifications, Announcements, Storage, ApiKeys, Inquiries). Confirmed zero references to "Modules.Configuration" or "Configuration" module anywhere under api/src.
  - Modules.Identity: functionally dead. The string is syntactically formed but the right-hand IsEnabledAsync call never executes for Identity because IsCore short-circuits it first. Confirmed no other code reads "Modules.Identity" or "\"Modules.Identity\"" anywhere under api/src (grepped).
  - Wallow:Modules block: confirmed zero readers anywhere in api/src (grep for "Wallow:Modules"/"Wallow__Modules"/GetSection("Wallow") returns nothing). The top-level "Wallow" JSON key contains ONLY "Modules" — safe to delete outright, nothing else lives under it.
  - The other 6 keys (Branding, Storage, Notifications, Announcements, Inquiries, ApiKeys) are genuinely read, once each, by the same loop.

=== 2. DECISION: Wallow-juxf is SUBSUMED — close as duplicate, with one condition ===

juxf's acceptance criteria are: (a) both Modules.Configuration keys removed, (b) fork-guide.md's example updated + stale-key caveat removed, (c) `./scripts/run-tests.sh api` green. All three fall out of this design's deliverables below — (a) is item 3.1, (b) is item 3.4, (c) is covered by the test plan (section 5). CONDITION: do not close juxf until this bead's fork-guide.md edit (3.4) actually lands — that is the one piece of juxf's own acceptance criteria that scul's original body did not explicitly name (scul's ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA talks only about appsettings*.json + the architecture test). I am widening scul's deliverables to include the doc edit specifically so the subsumption claim is true, not just asserted. Implementer: treat 3.4 below as mandatory for this bead, not optional polish.

=== 3. THE FIX (exact edits) ===

3.1. api/src/Wallow.Api/appsettings.json — in the FeatureManagement block (lines 80-89), delete the "Modules.Identity" and "Modules.Configuration" lines. Delete the entire "Wallow": { "Modules": {...} } object (lines 90-100) outright — it is dead code with the same phantom entry, and FeatureManagement is the one Microsoft.FeatureManagement's AddFeatureManagement() actually binds by convention. Result:
```
  "FeatureManagement": {
    "Modules.Branding": true,
    "Modules.Storage": true,
    "Modules.Notifications": true,
    "Modules.Announcements": true,
    "Modules.Inquiries": true,
    "Modules.ApiKeys": false
  },
```
(the block's own trailing comma at old line 89 already sits correctly before "Database" once the "Wallow" object is deleted — no other punctuation fix needed.)

3.2. api/src/Wallow.Api/appsettings.Production.json — same two deletions in its FeatureManagement block (lines 48-57); it has no separate Wallow:Modules block to remove (confirmed: grep for "\"Wallow\"" in this file only matches unrelated "DefaultFromName"/"ServiceName" string values, not a JSON key). Result: identical 6-key shape as 3.1 but Modules.ApiKeys: true (unchanged value, matches existing Production intent).

3.3. api/src/Wallow.Api/appsettings.Development.json — NO CHANGE. It only ever overrode "Modules.ApiKeys": true; that key still exists after 3.1/3.2, and Development never referenced Identity or Configuration.

3.4. docs/getting-started/fork-guide.md, "Configuring Modules" section (lines 313-348) — replace the example JSON (lines 321-334) with the real post-fix 6-key block from 3.1, delete the caveat sentence at line 346 ("The block above is the shipped file verbatim, including `Modules.Configuration`... Do not carry it into your fork.") since there is nothing stale left to warn about. Also correct line 348's claim while you're there: "When a module is disabled, its DI services, database migrations, API controllers, and Wolverine handlers are all excluded from the application" is FALSE for migrations — api/src/Wallow.MigrationService/ModuleMigrations.cs:15-18 documents deliberately: "The migration host does NOT honour feature flags — it migrates every module's schema whether or not the API will register that module, because a disabled module must still be able to come back on without a migration step." Reword line 348 to say DI services, HTTP controllers (now true post-3mbx.1) and Wolverine handlers are excluded, but the module's Postgres schema is still migrated by Wallow.MigrationService regardless — a fork disabling a module does not lose the ability to re-enable it without a manual migration step. This is directly relevant to section 4 below and was already going to need re-reading during this doc pass.

=== 4. WHAT A MODULE'S FLAG MEANS NOW (post 3mbx.1, uncommitted) ===

For a non-core module (all 6 remaining flagged ones), Modules.{Name}=false now means, simultaneously and only in Wallow.Api:
  1. No DI registration (module.AddServices never called — Program.cs:79-84 area / WallowModules.AddWallowModules).
  2. No Wolverine handler discovery (Program.cs:198, `enabledModules.SelectMany(module => module.HandlerAssemblies)` — a disabled module's assemblies never reach WolverineOptions).
  3. In the Testing environment only, no inline EF migration (WallowModules.RunTestMigrationsAsync takes enabledModules, not the full registry).
  4. AS OF 3mbx.1 (uncommitted): no HTTP surface at all. Program.cs:507-514 calls `WallowModules.RemoveDisabledModuleApiParts(manager, enabledModules)` inside ConfigureApplicationPartManager, pruning the disabled module's whole .Api ApplicationPart before routing/ApiExplorer/OpenAPI ever see it. Disabled = 404 + absent from the OpenAPI doc, not 500.
It does NOT gate: Wallow.MigrationService's migrations (ModuleMigrations.cs takes WallowModuleRegistry.All UNFILTERED, by design, exactly so a disabled module's schema is ready the moment it's re-enabled — see 3.4).

For a core module (today: only Identity), the flag is INERT — `IsCore || IsEnabledAsync(...)` never evaluates the right-hand side, so the key's presence or value has zero effect. This is why 3.1/3.2 delete Modules.Identity rather than "make WallowModules.cs honour it": IWallowModule.IsCore's own XML doc (api/src/Shared/Wallow.Shared.Infrastructure/Modules/IWallowModule.cs:26-29) already states the contract as "a core module ignores its feature flag — it is always registered." Honouring the flag would mean un-coring Identity, a materially larger architectural change than this bead's scope (multi-tenancy, auth, and every other module's schema assume Identity is always present — see api/CLAUDE.md's module list note). Delete, don't honour.

ApiKeys ships disabled by default (Modules.ApiKeys=false in appsettings.json, true in Production.json and Development.json). Checked git history for a rationale: the earliest commit adding a Modules.ApiKeys key (659128bb, "refactor: remove RabbitMQ, update shared infrastructure and API config") bundles it into an unrelated infra refactor with no explanatory commit body, and no doc anywhere states it was a deliberate security-review gate. I could find no evidence either way. RECOMMENDATION: do NOT flip this default as part of scul — changing a shipped default is a product/security decision (this module issues long-lived API keys) that deserves its own justification and its own bead, not a rider on a config-reconciliation chore. Leave appsettings.json's ApiKeys=false and appsettings.Production.json's ApiKeys=true exactly as they are; if the team wants ApiKeys on by default everywhere, file that separately.

=== 5. ENFORCEMENT — ONE mechanism, a new fast architecture test (not a runtime guard) ===

DECISION: add exactly one new test file, api/tests/Wallow.Architecture.Tests/Modules/ModuleFeatureFlagTests.cs, and no code-level runtime guard for this specific concern (contrast with WallowModules.EnsureEveryShippedModuleIsMapped, which DOES belong at runtime — see "why not a runtime guard" below).

Template precedent to copy: api/tests/Modules/Identity/Wallow.Identity.Tests/Infrastructure/DevelopmentIssuerOriginTests.cs. It already does exactly this shape — locate the solution root by walking up from AppContext.BaseDirectory until Wallow.slnx is found, then `new ConfigurationBuilder().AddJsonFile(realPath, optional: false).Build()` to load a REAL checked-in appsettings file through the same provider Program.cs uses, then assert on the resulting IConfiguration/typed values — not on file text. No [Trait("Category","Integration")] on that file (grepped, confirmed) — it runs in the fast tier today, so this new file will too.

Why this satisfies "no source-reading tests" despite touching appsettings*.json: this loads the file through .NET's actual JSON *configuration* provider (AddJsonFile), the identical mechanism the running host uses to consume it, and asserts on IConfiguration.GetSection(...).GetChildren() key names — never File.ReadAllText, never regex/text parsing, never an assertion about the file's textual shape. appsettings.json is a committed data contract, the same class of artifact the repo already carves out for TS (packages/sdk's openapi-regen.test.ts, packages/styles's theme-css.test.ts) — checking a data contract against the runtime state it's supposed to produce is exactly that pattern, on the C# side, via the framework's own config API instead of hand parsing.

```csharp
namespace Wallow.Architecture.Tests.Modules;

/// <summary>
/// Guards the correspondence between each shipped appsettings file's FeatureManagement
/// "Modules.*" keys and the modules WallowModuleRegistry.All actually ships. Loads the real file
/// through the same ConfigurationBuilder.AddJsonFile pipeline Program.cs uses, so this is a
/// runtime-configuration assertion, not a text/structure check — see
/// Wallow.Identity.Tests/Infrastructure/DevelopmentIssuerOriginTests.cs for the precedent.
/// </summary>
public sealed class ModuleFeatureFlagTests
{
    public static TheoryData<string> EnvironmentOverlays => new()
    {
        "appsettings.json",
        "appsettings.Development.json",
        "appsettings.Production.json",
        "appsettings.Staging.json",
        "appsettings.Testing.json",
    };

    [Theory]
    [MemberData(nameof(EnvironmentOverlays))]
    public void MergedConfiguration_DeclaresExactlyTheNonCoreRegistryModules(string overlay)
    {
        IConfiguration configuration = BuildMergedConfiguration(overlay);

        IEnumerable<string> declared = configuration
            .GetSection("FeatureManagement")
            .GetChildren()
            .Select(section => section.Key)
            .Where(key => key.StartsWith("Modules.", StringComparison.Ordinal))
            .Select(key => key["Modules.".Length..]);

        IEnumerable<string> expected = WallowModuleRegistry.All
            .Where(module => !module.IsCore)
            .Select(module => module.Name);

        declared.Should().BeEquivalentTo(expected,
            $"{overlay}'s merged FeatureManagement:Modules.* keys must name exactly the non-core " +
            "modules WallowModuleRegistry.All ships. A core module's flag is never evaluated " +
            "(ResolveEnabledModules short-circuits on IsCore), and a key naming no module at all " +
            "is dead weight — this is the Modules.Configuration / Modules.Identity defect.");
    }

    [Fact]
    public void BaseConfiguration_HasNoDuplicateModulesBlock()
    {
        IConfiguration configuration = BuildMergedConfiguration("appsettings.json");

        configuration.GetSection("Wallow").GetSection("Modules").GetChildren().Should().BeEmpty(
            "FeatureManagement is the only section AddFeatureManagement binds by convention — a " +
            "second, unread copy under Wallow:Modules is exactly the drift this test prevents; " +
            "it must never come back.");
    }

    private static IConfiguration BuildMergedConfiguration(string overlay)
    {
        string apiDirectory = Path.Combine(GetSolutionRoot(), "src", "Wallow.Api");
        ConfigurationBuilder builder = new();
        builder.AddJsonFile(Path.Combine(apiDirectory, "appsettings.json"), optional: false);
        if (overlay != "appsettings.json")
        {
            builder.AddJsonFile(Path.Combine(apiDirectory, overlay), optional: false);
        }

        return builder.Build();
    }

    private static string GetSolutionRoot()
    {
        DirectoryInfo? directory = new(AppContext.BaseDirectory);
        while (directory != null && !File.Exists(Path.Combine(directory.FullName, "Wallow.slnx")))
        {
            directory = directory.Parent;
        }

        return directory?.FullName ?? throw new InvalidOperationException("Solution root not found");
    }
}
```
(needs `using Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration;` and `using Wallow.Modules.Registry;`; System.IO/System.Linq come from ImplicitUsings.) Wallow.Architecture.Tests.csproj already ProjectReferences both Wallow.Api and Wallow.Modules.Registry (verified in the .csproj) — no project-file edit needed.

WHY NOT a runtime InvalidOperationException guard (the EnsureEveryShippedModuleIsMapped pattern) for this specific concern: EnsureEveryShippedModuleIsMapped guards a CODE-to-CODE correspondence (WallowModules._moduleApiAssemblies vs WallowModuleRegistry.All) where the two lists have no legitimate reason to ever differ, in any caller, including tests. The appsettings-to-registry correspondence is different: ModuleToggleTests.cs and ModuleRegistryTests.cs (api/tests/Wallow.Architecture.Tests/Modules/) both deliberately construct PARTIAL in-memory IConfiguration objects to unit-test AddWallowModules in isolation (e.g. ModuleToggleTests.OptionalModules_ShouldNotRegister_WhenFeatureFlagsDisabled sets only 2 of 6 optional-module keys, on purpose, to prove "absent flag = disabled"). Baking a "config must declare every key" invariant into AddWallowModules/ResolveEnabledModules itself would either false-positive on those legitimate partial configs or force rewriting them to always carry a complete flag set, losing their ability to test default-off behaviour in isolation. Checked: none of those existing tests declare a PHANTOM key (a name outside the 7-module registry), so even a phantom-only runtime guard would be redundant with the dedicated test below without adding real protection beyond it. Conclusion: put the invariant only where the real artifact lives — the dedicated test above, which never touches AddWallowModules and therefore cannot conflict with those unit tests' synthetic configs.

Confirmed zero edits needed to ModuleToggleTests.cs or ModuleRegistryTests.cs: both already use real module names only (including "Identity", which remains a valid module name — only its FILE presence in appsettings.json is being removed, not its status as a real, registry-listed module), so nothing in this design invalidates their existing assertions. This also answers scul's own NOTES coupling to Wallow-vmns.7 ("ModuleToggleTests hardcodes Modules.Identity... the two beads should agree on the key set"): ModuleToggleTests's in-memory dictionaries are synthetic per-test config, not the shipped file, and Wallow-vmns.7 is about deriving THEIR hardcoded module-name lists from the registry (a different, still-open, still-valid concern) — not about the shipped appsettings.json content this bead reconciles. No conflict; leave vmns.7 alone.

=== 6. TEST PLAN ===

Per .claude/rules/TESTING.md: `./scripts/run-tests.sh` (fast tier — the new ModuleFeatureFlagTests file has no Docker dependency and no [Trait("Category","Integration")], so it runs here). No new integration-tier test is needed from scul itself: the bead's acceptance criterion "the API starts with every module enabled and with each optional module disabled in turn" is ALREADY covered by 3mbx.1's uncommitted api/tests/Wallow.Api.Tests/Integration/DisabledModuleHttpSurfaceTests.cs (ApiKeys-disabled and Storage-disabled scenarios against the real WallowApiFactory). Run `./scripts/run-tests.sh` and `./scripts/run-tests.sh integration` (or `./scripts/run-tests.sh api integration` to scope it) once as a full-suite sanity check after 3.1-3.4 land, since a bad JSON edit here would surface as those integration tests failing to boot the host, not as a fast-tier failure. `./scripts/run-tests.sh arch` narrows to the new test alone during development.

