description:
Implement per-tenant rate limiting using ASP.NET Core 10's built-in rate limiter middleware combined with keyed DI services and route groups. Currently rate limiting (if any) is global — tenants should have independent rate limit buckets.

Steps:
1. Configure rate limiting in Program.cs with tenant-aware partition keys:
   ```csharp
   builder.Services.AddRateLimiter(options =>
   {
       options.AddFixedWindowLimiter("per-tenant", opt =>
       {
           opt.PermitLimit = 100;
           opt.Window = TimeSpan.FromMinutes(1);
       });
       options.OnRejected = (context, ct) => { /* return 429 ProblemDetails */ };
   });
   ```
2. Use RateLimitPartition.GetFixedWindowLimiter with TenantId as partition key
3. Apply to route groups or individual endpoints via .RequireRateLimiting()
4. Return 429 Too Many Requests in ProblemDetails format (consistent with error handling)
5. Consider different tiers per tenant (configurable via Configuration module)
6. Add rate limit headers (X-RateLimit-Limit, X-RateLimit-Remaining, Retry-After)

Note: TenantId is available via ITenantContext after TenantResolutionMiddleware runs. Rate limiter middleware must be placed AFTER tenant resolution in the pipeline.

Use the C# 14 semi-auto property 'field' keyword to clean up value object validation. Currently value objects use explicit backing fields or constructor-only validation. The field keyword allows inline validation in property setters with compiler-synthesized backing fields.

Example transformation:
```csharp
// Before (explicit backing)
public record Money
{
    public decimal Amount { get; }
    public string Currency { get; }

    private Money(decimal amount, string currency)
    {
        Amount = amount;
        Currency = currency;
    }
}

// After (field keyword for computed validation)
// Note: For records with primary constructors, the field keyword 
// is most useful in non-record value objects or for adding 
// validated settable properties
```

Candidates:
- Money (Billing) — amount validation
- EmailAddress (Communications) — format validation  
- PhoneNumber (Communications) — E.164 validation
- RetentionPolicy (Storage) — period validation
- VariantWeight (Configuration) — weight validation

Evaluate each value object and apply the field keyword where it reduces boilerplate.

